Chapter 1: Higan

The estate hummed quietly, the soft stirrings of life threading through its halls. Sunlight fell in muted gold across the polished floors, brushing the curves of the carved furniture, the shadows of drapes stretching lazily across the walls. In the nursery, a new presence stirred—a small, delicate figure, swaddled in soft white linen. Higan, the child of Leora and Sukuna, lay sleeping with the faintest rise and fall of his chest, already marked by the subtle, ominous pattern of red spider lilies along his shoulder and neck.

Tsukihiko hovered near the crib, eyes lingering on the infant with a mixture of awe and reverence. The baby’s tiny fingers flexed, and a soft sigh escaped from his lips, drawing Tsukihiko closer. He bent and brushed a hand over the downy hair, whispering in a voice only the baby could hear. “You’re mine to protect, little one. But don’t worry… I’ve got a bigger guardian here too.” His gaze flicked toward Chōbei, who was lounging on the bed nearby, one arm propped behind his head, the other drumming lightly against his chest.

Chōbei’s eyes lifted, amber catching the light, a slow, teasing smile spreading across his face. “You talk as if you’ve got me outnumbered, Moon Prince,” he said, voice low and playful. “Do I seem afraid of a little tiny human?”

“Not the human,” Tsukihiko corrected, the edge in his voice softening as he pressed closer, pressing his shoulder against Chōbei’s. “You. I’m not letting anyone else take you today.”

Chōbei chuckled, letting his hand curl around Tsukihiko’s wrist, holding him in place. He tilted his head, brushing a kiss along the corner of his mouth, lingering, daring. “Is that a challenge, or are you just clinging to me because you’re cold?”

Tsukihiko flushed, eyes narrowing. “Maybe both,” he admitted, moving to curl against Chōbei, pressing a hand to his chest. He nuzzled, letting himself relax, letting the warmth of Chōbei’s body chase away the lingering chills of worry. “Don’t leave me,” he murmured.

“I’m not going anywhere,” Chōbei promised, voice husky, fingers tracing teasing lines down Tsukihiko’s arm. “You know I love this part of you… clingy, demanding, always needing a bit more.”

Outside the nursery, Friedhelm moved with quiet grace. The uncle of the new baby, the brother who had shared so much with Leora, came in with a stack of folded blankets. His smile was warm, easy, the light of the room softening under his presence. “I see the usual chaos,” he said gently, setting the blankets down. “And yet somehow, everything is perfectly in place.”

Tsukihiko shot him a playful glare, holding Chōbei tighter. “You’re not interrupting, Uncle Friedhelm,” he said firmly.

Friedhelm chuckled. “I wouldn’t dream of it,” he said, though he stayed, eyes quietly observing the interplay, noticing Chōbei’s subtle teasing, the way Tsukihiko’s eyes sparkled with mischief and affection.

The door opened again, and Sukuna entered, imposing yet controlled, his presence drawing an immediate hush. He studied the scene, eyes lingering first on the child, then on the pair on the bed, before settling on Chōbei. There was a pause, deliberate, as Sukuna’s gaze pierced him. “What shall this one be named?” he asked, voice low and commanding, yet threaded with something heavier, almost ceremonial.

Chōbei’s amber eyes met his, calm, teasing, yet serious beneath the surface. “Higan,” he said softly, the weight of the choice deliberate. “It suits him.”

Sukuna’s expression shifted subtly—a nod, a slight tightening at the edges of his lips. The room felt heavier, charged. Even Friedhelm’s hand on a blanket faltered. Chōbei leaned against Tsukihiko, playful and teasing as always, but under that grin was the awareness of the gravity of naming a child born of such darkness and hope.

The day passed in quiet intimacy. Tsukihiko remained pressed against Chōbei, letting him tease, let go, and hold, a cocoon of warmth and closeness, while Higan slept in peace, unaware yet of the weight of the world surrounding him. Outside the nursery, Friedhelm moved between rooms, keeping watch, ensuring the fragile bubble of security remained unbroken.

And while the estate held its breaths, waiting, watching, the foundations of what was to come quietly set themselves—playful, charged, and deeply, irrevocably entwined.

